From 46c88dfe3c5f880f2f760ea3078b9058516deefc Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: reaper <theunbounddeveloper@outlook.com>
Date: Sun, 13 Sep 2026 13:15:52 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] firewall: a refused filter-table restore is loud and
 survivable (review R15 field data)

Field data from the RT-BE88U report (2026-09-13): the two copies of
/tmp/filter_rules that rule_apply_checking() saved at boot both pass
`iptables-restore --test`, and every `service restart_firewall` hours later
still leaves start_default_filter()'s boot skeleton in the filter table - no
port forwards, no VPN server chains, no SECURITY, no TCPMSS clamp. So the
refusal is the kernel's, deterministic, and of one line; and because _eval()
sends the restore's stdout and stderr to /dev/null, the router could not say
which line. The box ran that way for two weeks with a working nat table, so
forwarded flows were translated and then dropped.

rc/firewall.c: reaper_restore_rules() replaces the four filter restores
(v4 and v6, both filter_setting variants). It keeps the restore's own words
under /tmp/err_rules/<file>.err, names the refused line verbatim in syslog,
keeps the saved copy as before, and then applies the table WITHOUT that line,
up to eight times: a table missing one rule the kernel was never going to
enforce beats a skeleton that enforces none of them. A refusal that names no
line (a crash) is logged and left alone. The declaration, table and COMMIT
lines are never stripped.

rc/rwatch.c: check 3f reports `filter-skeleton` when INPUT_ICMP and SECURITY
- declared only by filter_setting() - are absent in router mode after boot
grace, logs once, and requests one restart_firewall per boot.

others/reaper_diag 1.3.16: 14f prints the table's state (full vs boot
skeleton), the /tmp/err_rules listing and each .err file's first line, with a
FINDING when the skeleton is what runs.

Ruled out on the owner's RT-BE96U before this was written: the kernel accepts
a `policy --dir out` match in an INPUT-reachable chain and a chain-jump loop
(both committed, exit 0), so the refused line is not of either class; the
`upnp` ipset rule is not compiled for any model; the TCPMSS clamp in
PControls is never jumped to. The line itself is still wanted from the
reporter's box; the next beta's syslog will carry it.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 release/src/router/others/reaper_diag |  13 ++-
 release/src/router/rc/firewall.c      | 132 ++++++++++++++++++++++++--
 release/src/router/rc/rwatch.c        |  29 ++++++
